  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This adapter is compiled only for unit tests. TermLike requires this\n callback, but our fixed-width, single-line displays do not move down.\n It is a no-op with no application behavior or untrusted-input path.\n Real terminal drawing is exercised by the PTY integration test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_down]` is a fenced exception with substantive commentary, and the source agrees with it.\n\n- **Test-only:** `mod[tests]` is `#[cfg(test)]`, and `RecordingTerm` is a test adapter. `move_cursor_down` is a no-op that returns `Ok(())`, so it has no application behavior or untrusted-input path.\n- **Single-line displays:** `Progress::with_target` in `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs` uses single-line templates, `{spinner} {msg} [{elapsed_precise}] ...`. Nothing in them moves the cursor down.\n- **PTY test:** `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` calls `openpty` and checks that progress updates in place with escape sequences. That supports the claim that real terminal drawing is exercised by a PTY integration test.\n\nThe citation is on an explicit function with a body, which the built-in allows. The explanation is not just \"difficult to test\", and no current source contradicts it.",
